{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer 1 (R1) in a peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Methods and Experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Temporal Label Models for Estimating Progress in LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM-based agents—where agents generate superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions during long-horizon tasks. The paper makes three contributions:\n\n1. **Temporal Label Model (TLM):** An HMM-based label aggregation method that models labeling function (LF) temporal dynamics—leading, concurrent, and lagging roles—for sequential weak supervision. On a 412-window human gold set (κ=0.82), TLM improves over majority vote by 12.2pp and FlyingSquid by 5.9pp.\n\n2. **ProgressProbe:** A lightweight GRU probe (847K params, ~3ms latency) trained on TLM labels achieves 0.858 macro-F1 on the gold set, outperforming the LF oracle by 6.0pp (\"student exceeds teacher\") and best LLM-as-judge (Claude-3.5-Sonnet CoT at 0.813) by 4.5pp while being 690× faster and 689× cheaper.\n\n3. **Cross-agent transfer:** SWE-agent→OpenHands zero-shot transfer: probe retains 0.791 F1 (−6.7pp) while LF oracle collapses to 0.582 (−21.6pp).\n\n## Method Details\n\n- Problem formulated as window-level binary classification over sliding windows of k=5 consecutive agent steps\n- 4 labeling functions: command_repetition, edit_churn, output_diversity, outcome_backpropagation\n- LF temporal dynamics measured over 3,847 stagnation onset windows (Table 1): command_repetition leads by −1.82 steps (p<0.001), outcome_backpropagation lags by +2.13 steps (p<0.001), others concurrent\n- TLM is an HMM with 2 hidden states {progress, stagnation}, transition probs P(stag|prog)=0.083, P(prog|stag)=0.142, trained via EM on 54,809 windows, ΔBIC=−342.7 vs static model\n- ProgressProbe: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B (d=1024) → single-layer GRU (hidden=256) → attention-weighted aggregation → linear classifier; optional SupCon auxiliary loss\n\n## Experimental Setup\n\n- Data: SWE-agent trajectories on SWE-bench, 54,809 windows total, conf≥2 subset = 15,401 windows\n- Cross-agent: 312 OpenHands trajectories, 8,934 windows\n- Gold set: 412 windows, 3 annotators, Fleiss' κ=0.823, 91% majority agreement\n- Baselines: Repeat-3, CosineSim, Mean-pool+LR/MLP, Structural GBDT, LF Oracle, FlyingSquid, LLM-as-Judge (GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet in zero/few/CoT)\n\n## Key Results Tables\n\n**Table 2 (Main Results on Gold Set):**\n- Repeat-3: 0.589, CosineSim: 0.583, Structural GBDT: 0.725, MV: 0.720, Mean-pool+LR: 0.782, Mean-pool+MLP: 0.796, FlyingSquid: 0.782, LF Oracle: 0.799, GPT-4o CoT: 0.794, Claude Sonnet CoT: 0.813, Probe (MV labels): 0.831, TLM: 0.841, **ProgressProbe (TLM labels): 0.858**\n\n**Table 3 (Label Model Ladder):**\n- MV: Gold F1=0.720, Conf=1 Acc=0.64, Probe↓F1=0.831\n- FlyingSquid: Gold F1=0.782, Conf=1 Acc=0.71, Probe↓F1=0.831\n- TLM: Gold F1=0.841, Conf=1 Acc=0.79, Probe↓F1=0.858\n\n**Table 4 (LLM-as-judge):**\nAll LLM configs at ~$4.82/1k and 2.1s latency; ProgressProbe: 0.858, 3ms, $0.007/1k\n\n**Table 5 (Cross-agent):**\n- Repeat-3: SWE 0.589 → OH 0.510 (Δ=−7.9)\n- LF Oracle: SWE 0.799 → OH 0.582 (Δ=−21.6)\n- ProgressProbe: SWE 0.858 → OH 0.791 (Δ=−6.7)\n\n**Table 6 (Architecture ablation on conf≥2):**\n- Mean-pool+LR: 0.844, Mean-pool+MLP: 0.857, GRU+mean-pool: 0.902, Transformer(4L): 0.908, GRU+attention: 0.919\n\n**Table 7 (Architecture with params):**\n- MLP pooled: 0.857, GRU+mean-pool: 0.902, Transformer(4L): 0.908/2.13M, GRU+attention: 0.919/847K\n\n**Table 8 (Encoder):** GTE: 0.885, E5-large: 0.898, BGE-M3: 0.903, Qwen3-0.6B: 0.919\n\n**Table 9 (LF hold-one-out on gold):** None: 0.858, outcome_bp: 0.846(−1.2), output_diversity: 0.845(−1.3), edit_churn: 0.837(−2.2), command_repetition: 0.820(−3.9), All except cmd_rep: 0.785(−7.4)\n\n**Table 10 (Data scaling):** 10%→0.823, 25%→0.871, 50%→0.902, 100%→0.919\n\n**Table 11 (Early termination):** N=3: 31.2% saved/94.7% preserved/5.3% false, N=4: 27.4%/96.8%/3.2%, N=5: 22.8%/97.9%/2.1%\n\n## Additional Results (from paper text)\n\n- Outcome leakage: holding out outcome_bp drops gold F1 by only 1.2pp (0.846 vs 0.858); last-3-windows-only variant: −0.4pp\n- Multi-seed: 0.919±0.011 on conf≥2, all seeds exceed every baseline by ≥5pp (p=0.008)\n- CosineSim: default threshold 0.313, optimized threshold 0.583, overlap coefficient 0.61\n- Full-data (conf≥1): 0.847 macro-F1, −5.9pp from conf≥2; stagnation-F1 more affected (−8.2pp vs −3.5pp)\n- Error analysis: 57 errors on gold (24 FP, 33 FN); FP: exploratory divergence before pivot; FN: semantic stagnation with diverse tokens; error rate: resolved 0.11 vs unresolved 0.19\n- Non-NLP structural baseline: best GBDT 0.725, combined structural+NL: 0.920 vs NL-only 0.919\n- Interpretability: UMAP silhouette 0.63 (0.71 with SupCon), attention concentrates on last 2 steps (~59%)\n- SupCon: +0.6pp F1 (0.925 vs 0.919), silhouette 0.71 vs 0.58\n\n## Imagined Experiments (from review_context.yaml) vs Paper Coverage\n\nThe review context lists 18 imagined experiments. Check which are covered and which are missing or under-reported:\n\n1. Multi-seed stability — covered (Section 5.6)\n2. CosineSim baseline diagnostic — covered (Section A.4)\n3. Full-data (conf≥1) training — covered (Section A.5)\n4. LF ensemble oracle baseline — covered (Table 2, Section 5.3)\n5. LLM-as-judge baseline — covered (Table 4, Section 5.4)\n6. Mean-pooling + LR baseline — covered (Table 2, Table 6)\n7. Error analysis — covered (Section 5.8)\n8. Cross-agent transfer — covered (Table 5, Section 5.5)\n9. Temporal label model — covered (Table 3, Section 5.2)\n10. FlyingSquid baseline — covered (Table 3)\n11. Outcome BP sensitivity — covered (Section 5.6)\n12. Window size ablation — covered (Section 5.6)\n13. Architecture ablation — covered (Table 6-7, Section 5.6)\n14. LF hold-one-out — covered (Table 9, Section 5.6)\n15. SupCon ablation — covered (Section A.7)\n16. Non-NLP structural baseline — covered (Section 5.7)\n17. Encoder ablation — covered (Table 8, Section 5.6)\n18. Human annotation gold set — covered (Section 4)\n19. LF temporal dynamics — covered (Table 1, Section 3.3)\n20. Data scaling — covered (Table 10, Section 5.6)\n21. Early termination simulation — covered (Table 11, Section 5.10)\n22. Interpretability visualization — covered (Section 5.9)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Methodological soundness** — Is the TLM formulation rigorous? Are there hidden assumptions? Is the HMM appropriate for this setting?\n2. **Experimental rigor** — Are experiments well-designed? Are comparisons fair? Are there confounds? Statistical validity?\n3. **Reproducibility** — Is there enough detail to reproduce? Missing hyperparameters, preprocessing steps?\n4. **Evaluation concerns** — Is the gold set evaluation sound? Any circular evaluation risks? Is the cross-agent evaluation convincing?\n5. **Ablation completeness** — Are the ablations sufficient to support the claims?\n6. **Weaknesses and missing experiments** — What's missing? What would strengthen the paper?\n\nOutput your review in this EXACT format:\n\n```\n## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary of the paper's approach and main findings]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions]\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n[What's missing that would strengthen the paper]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5, where 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Score\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nScore: X — [one sentence justification]\n```\n"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SLbL3N6wstXn9xqWvryVSC","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"d864ed43-cd72-434c-85a4-80c48a25d968","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:40:00.585Z"}
