{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VadYuhUaWqVUQuTrU5PKvB","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Temporal Label Models for Estimating Progress in LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM-based agents—where agents generate superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions during long-horizon tasks. The paper makes three contributions:\n\n1. **Temporal Label Model (TLM):** An HMM-based label aggregation method that models labeling function (LF) temporal dynamics—leading, concurrent, and lagging roles—for sequential weak supervision. On a 412-window human gold set (κ=0.82), TLM improves over majority vote by 12.2pp and FlyingSquid by 5.9pp.\n\n2. **ProgressProbe:** A lightweight GRU probe (847K params, ~3ms latency) trained on TLM labels achieves 0.858 macro-F1 on the gold set, outperforming the LF oracle by 6.0pp (\"student exceeds teacher\") and best LLM-as-judge (Claude-3.5-Sonnet CoT at 0.813) by 4.5pp while being 690× faster and 689× cheaper.\n\n3. **Cross-agent transfer:** SWE-agent→OpenHands zero-shot transfer: probe retains 0.791 F1 (−6.7pp) while LF oracle collapses to 0.582 (−21.6pp).\n\n## Method Details\n\n- Problem formulated as window-level binary classification over sliding windows of k=5 consecutive agent steps\n- 4 labeling functions: command_repetition, edit_churn, output_diversity, outcome_backpropagation\n- LF temporal dynamics measured over 3,847 stagnation onset windows: command_repetition leads by −1.82 steps (p<0.001), outcome_backpropagation lags by +2.13 steps (p<0.001)\n- TLM is an HMM with 2 hidden states, trained via EM on 54,809 windows\n- ProgressProbe: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B → GRU → attention → classifier\n\n## Related Work (from paper)\n\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Agent monitoring and failure detection:** Cycle detection (Zhuge et al. 2024, Zhang et al. 2024), TrajAD (Li et al. 2024), self-reflection (Shinn et al. 2023, Yao et al. 2024, Hao et al. 2023)\n2. **Process reward models:** Lightman et al. 2023, Wang et al. 2024a, Uesato et al. 2022 — step-level correctness requiring human annotations\n3. **Data programming and weak supervision:** Snorkel (Ratner et al. 2017), FlyingSquid (Fu et al. 2020) — assumes i.i.d., TLM extends to sequential\n4. **LLM-as-judge:** Zheng et al. 2024, DuBois et al. 2024\n\n## Key Claims\n\n1. LFs on sequential agent data violate i.i.d. assumptions, exhibiting systematic temporal roles (leading/lagging)\n2. TLM leverages these temporal dynamics for better label aggregation\n3. Probe trained on TLM labels surpasses LF oracle (\"student exceeds teacher\") and LLM-as-judge at 690× lower latency\n4. Probe captures generalizable progress signals that transfer across agents\n\n## Discussion and Limitations (from paper)\n\n- TLM principle applies broadly to any weak supervision on sequential data\n- Limitations: (1) only 2 SE agents tested, (2) hand-crafted LFs, (3) window-level can't distinguish productive exploration from aimless wandering, (4) gold set covers one domain\n\n## Full Results\n\n**Table 2 (Main Results on Gold Set):**\nRepeat-3: 0.589, CosineSim: 0.583, Structural GBDT: 0.725, MV: 0.720, Mean-pool+LR: 0.782, Mean-pool+MLP: 0.796, FlyingSquid: 0.782, LF Oracle: 0.799, GPT-4o CoT: 0.794, Claude Sonnet CoT: 0.813, Probe (MV labels): 0.831, TLM: 0.841, **ProgressProbe (TLM labels): 0.858**\n\n**Table 5 (Cross-agent transfer):**\n- Repeat-3: SWE 0.589 → OH 0.510 (Δ=−7.9)\n- LF Oracle: SWE 0.799 → OH 0.582 (Δ=−21.6)\n- ProgressProbe: SWE 0.858 → OH 0.791 (Δ=−6.7)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment** — How novel is each contribution? Is TLM a straightforward HMM application or a genuine methodological advance? Is the \"student exceeds teacher\" finding novel or expected?\n2. **Related work completeness** — Are important baselines or prior works missing? Particularly:\n   - Temporal extensions of data programming / weak supervision\n   - Agent trajectory analysis methods\n   - HMM-based label models in other domains\n   - Process supervision and reward modeling\n   - Stagnation/loop detection in software agents\n3. **Positioning and differentiation** — Is the paper well-positioned relative to prior work? Are the distinctions clear and fair?\n4. **Significance and impact** — How significant are the contributions to the broader ML community? Is the problem important enough?\n5. **Generalizability concerns** — How well do the claims generalize beyond this specific setting?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity** — Is the paper well-written? Are claims supported?\n\nOutput your review in this EXACT format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary focusing on the novelty claims]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers or research areas not cited that should be]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5, where 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Score\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nScore: X — [one sentence justification]\n```\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14371,"cache_read_input_tokens":41357,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14371},"output_tokens":17,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"07a77ccc-98ba-4f42-80b4-2dd37e2f5f04"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"aa0f98637551edcfc","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer 2 (R2) in a peer review for a top ML venue (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject … 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **Novelty and Related Work**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title:** Temporal Label Models for Estimating Progress in LLM Agent Trajectories\n\nThe paper addresses \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM-based agents—where agents generate superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions during long-horizon tasks. The paper makes three contributions:\n\n1. **Temporal Label Model (TLM):** An HMM-based label aggregation method that models labeling function (LF) temporal dynamics—leading, concurrent, and lagging roles—for sequential weak supervision. On a 412-window human gold set (κ=0.82), TLM improves over majority vote by 12.2pp and FlyingSquid by 5.9pp.\n\n2. **ProgressProbe:** A lightweight GRU probe (847K params, ~3ms latency) trained on TLM labels achieves 0.858 macro-F1 on the gold set, outperforming the LF oracle by 6.0pp (\"student exceeds teacher\") and best LLM-as-judge (Claude-3.5-Sonnet CoT at 0.813) by 4.5pp while being 690× faster and 689× cheaper.\n\n3. **Cross-agent transfer:** SWE-agent→OpenHands zero-shot transfer: probe retains 0.791 F1 (−6.7pp) while LF oracle collapses to 0.582 (−21.6pp).\n\n## Method Details\n\n- Problem formulated as window-level binary classification over sliding windows of k=5 consecutive agent steps\n- 4 labeling functions: command_repetition, edit_churn, output_diversity, outcome_backpropagation\n- LF temporal dynamics measured over 3,847 stagnation onset windows: command_repetition leads by −1.82 steps (p<0.001), outcome_backpropagation lags by +2.13 steps (p<0.001)\n- TLM is an HMM with 2 hidden states, trained via EM on 54,809 windows\n- ProgressProbe: Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B → GRU → attention → classifier\n\n## Related Work (from paper)\n\nThe paper covers 4 areas:\n1. **Agent monitoring and failure detection:** Cycle detection (Zhuge et al. 2024, Zhang et al. 2024), TrajAD (Li et al. 2024), self-reflection (Shinn et al. 2023, Yao et al. 2024, Hao et al. 2023)\n2. **Process reward models:** Lightman et al. 2023, Wang et al. 2024a, Uesato et al. 2022 — step-level correctness requiring human annotations\n3. **Data programming and weak supervision:** Snorkel (Ratner et al. 2017), FlyingSquid (Fu et al. 2020) — assumes i.i.d., TLM extends to sequential\n4. **LLM-as-judge:** Zheng et al. 2024, DuBois et al. 2024\n\n## Key Claims\n\n1. LFs on sequential agent data violate i.i.d. assumptions, exhibiting systematic temporal roles (leading/lagging)\n2. TLM leverages these temporal dynamics for better label aggregation\n3. Probe trained on TLM labels surpasses LF oracle (\"student exceeds teacher\") and LLM-as-judge at 690× lower latency\n4. Probe captures generalizable progress signals that transfer across agents\n\n## Discussion and Limitations (from paper)\n\n- TLM principle applies broadly to any weak supervision on sequential data\n- Limitations: (1) only 2 SE agents tested, (2) hand-crafted LFs, (3) window-level can't distinguish productive exploration from aimless wandering, (4) gold set covers one domain\n\n## Full Results\n\n**Table 2 (Main Results on Gold Set):**\nRepeat-3: 0.589, CosineSim: 0.583, Structural GBDT: 0.725, MV: 0.720, Mean-pool+LR: 0.782, Mean-pool+MLP: 0.796, FlyingSquid: 0.782, LF Oracle: 0.799, GPT-4o CoT: 0.794, Claude Sonnet CoT: 0.813, Probe (MV labels): 0.831, TLM: 0.841, **ProgressProbe (TLM labels): 0.858**\n\n**Table 5 (Cross-agent transfer):**\n- Repeat-3: SWE 0.589 → OH 0.510 (Δ=−7.9)\n- LF Oracle: SWE 0.799 → OH 0.582 (Δ=−21.6)\n- ProgressProbe: SWE 0.858 → OH 0.791 (Δ=−6.7)\n\n## Your Review Task\n\nProvide a thorough review focusing on:\n\n1. **Novelty assessment** — How novel is each contribution? Is TLM a straightforward HMM application or a genuine methodological advance? Is the \"student exceeds teacher\" finding novel or expected?\n2. **Related work completeness** — Are important baselines or prior works missing? Particularly:\n   - Temporal extensions of data programming / weak supervision\n   - Agent trajectory analysis methods\n   - HMM-based label models in other domains\n   - Process supervision and reward modeling\n   - Stagnation/loop detection in software agents\n3. **Positioning and differentiation** — Is the paper well-positioned relative to prior work? Are the distinctions clear and fair?\n4. **Significance and impact** — How significant are the contributions to the broader ML community? Is the problem important enough?\n5. **Generalizability concerns** — How well do the claims generalize beyond this specific setting?\n6. **Writing quality and clarity** — Is the paper well-written? Are claims supported?\n\nOutput your review in this EXACT format:\n\n```\n## R2 Review: Novelty and Related Work\n\n### Summary\n[2-3 sentence summary focusing on the novelty claims]\n\n### Strengths\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Weaknesses\n[Numbered list, each with a short bold title and 1-2 sentence explanation]\n\n### Questions for Authors\n[Numbered list of specific questions]\n\n### Missing Related Work\n[Specific papers or research areas not cited that should be]\n\n### Minor Issues\n[Typos, presentation issues, etc.]\n\n### Confidence Score\n[1-5, where 5=very confident]\n\n### Preliminary Score\n[1-6 scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept]\nScore: X — [one sentence justification]\n```\n","uuid":"74a7340e-afed-4994-b1ff-946d5a6be6d5","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664"}
