{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01WAe7bcKyEW7DWPJnCBsG99","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T081656\n3\ttier: median\n4\toverall_score: weak reject\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T09:03:22.254212+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t# Dream Review Report: Weakly-Supervised Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n9\t\n10\t**Dream ID:** 20260514T081656\n11\t**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n12\t**Date:** 2026-05-14\n13\t\n14\t---\n15\t\n16\t## 1. Paper Summary\n17\t\n18\tThe paper introduces a weak-supervision framework for detecting \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories — where agents take superficially diverse but collectively unproductive actions. Three domain-specific labeling functions (command repetition, file-diff delta, outcome back-propagation) produce noisy window-level labels via majority vote with confidence scores. A lightweight GRU probe (542K params) trained on high-confidence labels (conf≥2) achieves 0.906 macro-F1 on auto-labeled data, outperforming the Repeat-3 heuristic by 6.2pp. On a 400-window human gold set (Fleiss κ=0.62), the probe reaches 0.77 F1 — above the LF oracle (0.75) and Repeat-3 (0.72), but below an LLM-as-judge baseline (0.82 F1) that costs 35× more. Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands yields 0.73 F1 zero-shot, recoverable to 0.82 with 200 labeled examples. The paper positions the weak-supervision framework — not the probe — as the primary contribution.\n19\t\n20\t---\n21\t\n22\t## 2. Reviewer Summaries\n23\t\n24\t### Reviewer 1 (R1): Methods & Experiments — Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5\n25\t\n26\tR1 finds the experimental program impressively broad (16 experiments covering baselines, ablations, transfer, error analysis, interpretability, and deployment simulation). Instance-level data splitting correctly prevents cross-contamination. The \"student exceeds teacher\" result (+2pp over LF oracle on gold) and cost-accuracy Pareto analysis against LLM judges are highlighted as strengths.\n27\t\n28\tKey concerns: (1) The paper describes a GRU with learned attention (Eq. 1 + attention equations), but R1 questions whether the actual implementation matches this description, noting that all attention-based interpretability claims depend on this being accurate. (2) All results come from a single random seed with no variance reporting or significance tests; margins of 2-5pp on N=400 gold set likely fall within bootstrap confidence intervals. (3) The frozen sentence encoder producing 1024-dim embeddings is never named, and no encoder ablation exists. (4) The outcome_backpropagation LF may leak trajectory-level outcome information into window-level labels. (5) The gold set (N=400) is undersized for the narrow margins reported.\n29\t\n30\t### Reviewer 2 (R2): Novelty & Related Work — Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence: 4/5\n31\t\n32\tR2 finds the problem identification practically valuable and the experimental coverage thorough. The cost-efficiency analysis (35× cheaper, 930× faster than LLM judges) gives immediate practical relevance. The error overlap analysis (187 windows probe catches that Repeat-3 misses vs. 34 reverse) is convincing evidence of complementary signal.\n33\t\n34\tKey concerns: (1) Novelty is thin — majority vote aggregation and GRU classification are both off-the-shelf; no methodological innovation in adapting data programming to sequences. (2) Related work is severely incomplete (~10 references), missing Process Reward Models (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), agent scaffolding (Reflexion, LATS), and advanced label models (FlyingSquid). (3) Domain specificity undermines generality claims — all LFs, evaluation, and application are SWE-bench-specific. (4) The LLM-as-Judge comparison may undersell the LLM baseline (single prompt template, no chain-of-thought). (5) \"Semantic stagnation\" potentially rebrands existing concepts without sufficient justification for new terminology.\n35\t\n36\t---\n37\t\n38\t## 3. Roundtable Convergence\n39\t\n40\t### 3.1 Points of Agreement (High Confidence)\n41\t\n42\tBoth reviewers converge on these findings:\n43\t\n44\t| # | Issue | Severity | Agreement |\n45\t|---|-------|----------|-----------|\n46\t| 1 | Architecture description accuracy — paper describes GRU+attention but implementation details need verification | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n47\t| 2 | Single seed, no variance or significance tests | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n48\t| 3 | Embedding model unnamed, no encoder ablation | Critical | Both R1, R2 |\n49\t| 4 | outcome_backpropagation LF may leak trajectory-level outcome | Major | Both R1, R2 |\n50\t| 5 | Gold set (N=400) underpowered for reported margins | Major | Both R1, R2 |\n51\t| 6 | Novelty below top-venue bar (off-the-shelf components) | Major | R2 primary, R1 concurs |\n52\t| 7 | Related work severely incomplete | Major | R2 primary |\n53\t| 8 | Domain-specific LFs limit generality claims | Moderate | R2 primary |\n54\t| 9 | No cross-agent human validation (OpenHands) | Moderate | R1 primary |\n55\t\n56\t### 3.2 Points of Agreement — Strengths\n57\t\n58\t| # | Strength | Agreement |\n59\t|---|----------|-----------|\n60\t| 1 | Exceptional experimental breadth (16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations) | Both |\n61\t| 2 | \"Student exceeds teacher\" — probe outperforms its own label source on gold | Both |\n62\t| 3 | Cost-accuracy Pareto positioning vs. LLM judges is compelling and practical | Both |\n63\t| 4 | Error analysis with FP/FN taxonomy and overlap quantification | Both |\n64\t| 5 | LF temporal dynamics (leading/synchronous/lagging indicators) | Both |\n65\t| 6 | Honest framing — architecture as \"engineering choice, not contribution\" | Both |\n66\t| 7 | Instance-level data splitting prevents cross-contamination | Both |\n67\t| 8 | Data scaling curve establishes minimum viable dataset size (~4K windows) | R1 |\n68\t\n69\t### 3.3 Resolved Disagreements\n70\t\n71\tNo score disagreements to resolve. R1 and R2 independently arrived at 3/6 with complementary but non-conflicting rationales. R1's statistical rigor concerns and R2's novelty concerns reinforce each other: thin novelty places a higher burden on experimental execution, and the execution gaps (single seed, no CIs, small gold set) fail to meet that raised bar.\n72\t\n73\t---\n74\t\n75\t## 4. Consolidated Assessment\n76\t\n77\t### 4.1 Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n78\t\n79\t### 4.2 Confidence: 4/5 (High)\n80\t\n81\t### 4.3 Justification\n82\t\n83\tThe paper tackles a genuine and practically important problem — semantic stagnation in LLM agents is a real failure mode that existing tools detect poorly. The experimental program is among the most thorough we see at this stage, covering 16 experiments with good coverage of baselines, ablations, transfer evaluation, error analysis, and practical deployment simulation. The \"student exceeds teacher\" finding and the cost-accuracy Pareto positioning against LLM judges are genuinely useful contributions.\n84\t\n85\tHowever, the paper does not meet the acceptance bar for a top ML venue (~25% acceptance rate) for the following reasons:\n86\t\n87\t1. **Novelty gap.** The methodological contribution reduces to applying two well-known techniques (Snorkel-style majority vote + GRU classifier) to a new domain. The data programming framework is used without modification — no temporal label model, no LF dependency modeling, no innovation in aggregation for sequential data. This places the paper in \"competent application\" territory, which requires exceptionally strong empirical results to compensate.\n88\t\n89\t2. **Statistical rigor below threshold.** The compensating empirical strength is undermined by: single-seed results throughout, no confidence intervals or significance tests, and a gold set (N=400) that provides ~±3.5pp bootstrap CIs — meaning the 2pp probe-vs-oracle gap and several other key margins are likely non-significant. For a paper whose value proposition is empirical, this is disqualifying.\n90\t\n91\t3. **Architecture description concerns.** The paper describes a GRU with learned attention weights and builds interpretability arguments on attention patterns (concentration at stagnation onset, entropy differences). If the actual implementation differs from this description, the interpretability claims would be unsupported. This needs clarification.\n92\t\n93\t4. **Related work gaps.** ~10 references is far below venue expectations. The omission of Process Reward Models, agent scaffolding systems (Reflexion, LATS), and advanced label models (FlyingSquid) leaves the paper poorly positioned. The relationship to PRMs — which also estimate step-level quality in sequential decision-making — is never discussed.\n94\t\n95\t5. **Information leakage concern.** The outcome_backpropagation LF uses trajectory-level outcome to label individual windows, potentially allowing the probe to learn trajectory-position and exit-status proxies rather than genuine window-level progress signals. The paper does not quantify or control for this.\n96\t\n97\t### 4.4 Relationship to Experimental Design\n98\t\n99\tThe paper covers all experiments from the experimental design: full-data training, LF oracle, LLM-as-judge, MeanPool+LR, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, window size ablation, architecture ablation, LF hold-one-out, SupCon ablation, non-NLP baseline, data scaling, early termination simulation, interpretability analysis, human gold set, and LF temporal dynamics. Coverage is complete and well-organized across main text and appendix.\n100\t\n101\tThe experimental results are internally consistent with the design expectations. Key numbers closely match planned values (e.g., conf≥2 F1=0.906, full-data F1=0.82, zero-shot transfer F1=0.73, gold F1=0.77). The narrative adjustment from \"probe is strong\" to \"framework enables research, probe is one efficient downstream application\" is executed as designed.\n102\t\n103\t---\n104\t\n105\t## 5. Actionable Recommendations for Revision\n106\t\n107\t### Must-fix (for any resubmission):\n108\t\n109\t1. **Multi-seed results.** Run all main experiments with ≥3 seeds. Report mean ± std. Provide bootstrap CIs on gold-set comparisons.\n110\t\n111\t2. **Name the embedding model.** State the frozen encoder explicitly. Run at least one alternative encoder (e.g., BGE, E5, GTE) to assess sensitivity.\n112\t\n113\t3. **Clarify architecture.** Ensure the paper description exactly matches the implementation. If attention is used, the code should reflect it. If not, remove attention-based interpretability claims and rerun architecture ablations.\n114\t\n115\t4. **Expand related work.** Add PRMs (Lightman et al., Math-Shepherd), agent scaffolding (Reflexion, LATS, Tree of Thoughts), advanced label models (FlyingSquid), and execution feedback systems. Discuss relationship to PRMs explicitly. Target 30+ references.\n116\t\n117\t5. **Quantify outcome_backpropagation impact.** Report what fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories. Show results with outcome_backpropagation restricted to last-N-windows only (no blanket labeling).\n118\t\n119\t### Should-fix:\n120\t\n121\t6. **Larger gold set.** Scale to ≥1000 windows to tighten CIs on the 2-5pp margins that drive key claims.\n122\t\n123\t7. **Stronger LLM baseline.** Test chain-of-thought prompting for LLM-as-judge. The current single-template comparison may undersell the LLM.\n124\t\n125\t8. **Soft-match Repeat-3.** Implement edit-distance or embedding-similarity variant of Repeat-3 to make the comparison fairer.\n126\t\n127\t9. **Cross-domain signal.** Even a small-scale evaluation on non-SWE-bench trajectories (web navigation, research tasks) would substantially strengthen the generality claim.\n128\t\n129\t10. **Discuss PRM relationship.** Frame ProgressProbe relative to PRMs — what does weak supervision on trajectory structure buy over training a PRM with outcome supervision?\n130\t\n131\t### Nice-to-have:\n132\t\n133\t11. Consider using Snorkel's generative label model or FlyingSquid instead of naive majority vote, given low inter-LF agreement (LF agreement at onset = 0.35).\n134\t\n135\t12. Explore temporal confidence weighting (weight by LF lead/lag position rather than static confidence scores).\n136\t\n137\t---\n138\t\n139\t## 6. Meta-Assessment\n140\t\n141\tThis paper is a solid empirical study of a real problem, held back by insufficient novelty for a top venue and execution gaps in statistical rigor. The experimental design is comprehensive and the framing is honest. With the multi-seed results, named encoder, corrected architecture description, and expanded related work, it could become a competitive submission — likely as an empirical/applications paper rather than a methods paper. The weak-supervision framework for trajectory annotation is a genuine practical contribution even if it doesn't clear a high novelty bar; the right venue and framing could make this publishable.\n142\t\n143\t**Recommended venue positioning:** Workshop paper at a top venue (NeurIPS/ICML workshop on agents or data-centric AI), or full paper at a mid-tier venue (AAMAS, EACL). For top-venue acceptance, a methodological contribution to the label aggregation step (temporal label model) would be needed.\n144\t\n145\t---\n146\t\n147\t*Review conducted by: R1 (Methods & Experiments) + R2 (Novelty & Related Work)*\n148\t*Roundtable: 4-step protocol (Independent → Cross-share → Convergence → Synthesis)*\n149\t*Consensus score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Confidence 4/5*"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"fe80575c-03c6-4335-91e8-924c802c886b","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:41:09.691Z"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_progress","task_id":"aa0f98637551edcfc","tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","description":"Reading artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T081656/best/review_report.md","usage":{"total_tokens":45607,"tool_uses":17,"duration_ms":45778},"last_tool_name":"Read","uuid":"3111b800-5fa5-48ac-a295-28deb0e2ef92","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01C5gXW85iNpttiTtW7f5VLY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Bgo8mCSXfHDKhR3nfVoBm1","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0513/projects/agent_progress_probe/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260514T081656/best/review_report.md"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":345,"cache_read_input_tokens":44897,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":345,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":66,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"42f8db92-e935-4420-942e-5cdbb944ce58"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Bgo8mCSXfHDKhR3nfVoBm1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tdream_id: dream_20260514T081656\n3\ttier: best\n4\toverall_score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n5\ttimestamp: 2026-05-14T09:08:50.116011+00:00\n6\t---\n7\t\n8\t\n9\t# Reviewer 2 (R2) Review — Novelty & Related Work Focus\n10\t\n11\t**Paper:** ProgressProbe: Weakly-Supervised Temporal Progress Estimation for LLM Agent Trajectories\n12\t\n13\t---\n14\t\n15\t## Summary\n16\t\n17\tThe paper proposes ProgressProbe, a framework combining data-programming weak supervision (three labeling functions + majority vote) with a GRU probe to detect \"semantic stagnation\" in LLM agent trajectories at the window level. On SWE-bench trajectories, the probe achieves 0.906 macro-F1 on majority-vote labels and 0.862 on a 400-window human gold set, outperforming heuristic baselines, LF oracles, and LLM-as-Judge while being orders of magnitude cheaper. Cross-agent transfer from SWE-agent to OpenHands retains 0.835 F1.\n18\t\n19\t---\n20\t\n21\t## Strengths\n22\t\n23\t- **Practically relevant problem.** Semantic stagnation — where agents execute diverse but collectively ineffective actions — is a genuine and costly failure mode in deployed LLM agent systems. The problem formulation at the window level (rather than step level) is well-motivated by the observation that stagnation is a temporal phenomenon requiring multi-step context.\n24\t\n25\t- **\"Student exceeds teacher\" result is the cleanest contribution.** The +4.43pp gain over the LF oracle on conf>=2 data and +4.36pp on the human gold set demonstrates that the GRU learns temporal dependencies beyond the rules that generated its training signal. This is a non-trivial empirical finding that validates the data-programming-then-probe pipeline.\n26\t\n27\t- **Strong cost-efficiency argument.** The comparison against LLM-as-Judge (Claude Sonnet 4, GPT-4o) with explicit cost/latency quantification (31x cheaper, 606x faster, still +3.74pp higher F1) provides immediate practical relevance. The Pareto analysis is well-executed.\n28\t\n29\t- **Cross-agent transfer provides genuine generalization evidence.** The zero-shot SWE-agent-to-OpenHands evaluation (0.835 F1, +4.53pp over Repeat-3) with the accompanying analysis of distribution shift is more convincing than most transfer experiments in this space, particularly the observation that the LF oracle degrades severely (+0.87pp over Repeat-3) while the probe maintains a meaningful advantage.\n30\t\n31\t- **Comprehensive experimental coverage.** 16 experiments, 7 baselines, 6 ablations, error analysis, cross-agent transfer, LF temporal dynamics, and a downstream application — this exceeds the thoroughness of most submissions. The human gold set as primary evaluation, instance-level splitting, and circularity-aware experimental design show methodological care.\n32\t\n33\t- **Temporal vs. static aggregation gap is decisive.** The 13.72pp gap between GRU and mean-pool+LR, combined with the observation that even Repeat-3 outperforms mean-pooling, makes a compelling case that temporal modeling — not merely better features — drives the improvement. This is the strongest internal evidence for the paper's second claim.\n34\t\n35\t---\n36\t\n37\t## Weaknesses\n38\t\n39\t### Critical\n40\t\n41\t1. **Novelty is thin — both components are straightforward applications of existing techniques.**\n42\t   - The weak-supervision component is vanilla Snorkel majority voting with three hand-written threshold/pattern heuristics. No label model is used (Snorkel's generative model, MeTaL, FlyingSquid). The \"transfer of Snorkel to sequential data\" claim is undercut by the fact that the sequential aspect is handled entirely by the downstream GRU, not by any adaptation to the data programming framework itself. The LFs themselves are applied independently to each window — there is no modeling of LF temporal dependencies in the label aggregation step.\n43\t   - The GRU probe is a standard 2-layer recurrent classifier with attention over frozen embeddings. The architecture is correctly positioned as \"not a contribution,\" but this means the paper's novelty rests entirely on (a) the combination, which is an engineering contribution, and (b) the empirical findings. For a top venue, this demands exceptional experimental rigor (see R1's concerns about code-paper discrepancy and single-seed evaluation).\n44\t   - The \"first to apply data programming to sequential agent trajectory data\" is a narrow priority claim. Applying an existing tool to a new dataset is not, by itself, a methodological advance. What would elevate this is a temporal label model that accounts for LF dynamics — but this is deferred to future work.\n45\t\n46\t2. **Related work is severely incomplete — 10 references for a top-venue submission.**\n47\t   A competitive submission in this area requires engagement with 30-50 references. Critical missing areas:\n48\t   \n49\t   - **Process Reward Models (PRMs):** Lightman et al. (2023) \"Let's Verify Step by Step,\" Math-Shepherd (Wang et al. 2024), OmegaPRM. These estimate step-level correctness/progress in reasoning chains — conceptually the same task as ProgressProbe. The paper never addresses how window-level progress estimation differs from or improves upon step-level PRMs aggregated over windows. This is the single largest gap in the related work.\n50\t   \n51\t   - **RL value functions and reward modeling:** Any RL agent framework learns a value function that estimates expected future reward — this is literally a progress estimator. CodeRL (Le et al. 2022), RLHF-based agent training, outcome-based reward models. The paper should position ProgressProbe relative to value function estimation.\n52\t   \n53\t   - **Agent scaffolding with self-reflection and self-repair:** Reflexion (Shinn et al. 2023), LATS (Zhou et al. 2023), Tree of Thoughts (Yao et al. 2024). These detect agent failure and intervene — directly competing approaches to the same problem.\n54\t   \n55\t   - **Weak supervision beyond Snorkel:** MeTaL (Ratner et al. 2019 — cited but only as \"extensions\"), FlyingSquid (Fu et al. 2020), WRENCH benchmark (Zhang et al. 2021), programmatic labeling for structured/sequential data. The paper cites Ratner et al. 2019 generically but does not discuss why a label model was not used.\n56\t   \n57\t   - **Trajectory-level evaluation and credit assignment:** SWE-TRACE is cited but the broader literature on trajectory evaluation (MINT, AgentBench, reward redistribution methods) is absent.\n58\t   \n59\t   - **Noisy label learning:** The paper trains on labels with ~15-25% noise rate. The noisy label literature (mixup, co-teaching, DivideMix) is directly relevant and entirely absent.\n60\t   \n61\t   - **Active learning as alternative to weak supervision:** With a 400-window gold set already available, active learning could iteratively select the most informative windows for human annotation. This alternative paradigm is not discussed.\n62\t\n63\t### Major\n64\t\n65\t3. **\"Semantic stagnation\" may rebrand existing concepts without adequate differentiation.** The RL literature discusses \"plateaus\" in learning curves; the agent literature discusses \"looping,\" \"unproductive exploration,\" and \"exploration-exploitation failure.\" The paper introduces \"semantic stagnation\" as if it were a new phenomenon but does not argue why it requires a new term or how it relates to existing conceptualizations. Section 1 defines it as \"surface-level diversity that masks underlying ineffectiveness\" — this is precisely the exploration-exploitation dilemma in a different vocabulary. The paper would be strengthened by explicitly mapping semantic stagnation onto the existing taxonomy of agent failure modes and arguing for the specific gap it fills.\n66\t\n67\t4. **Domain specificity undermines generality claims.** All three LFs are hand-crafted for SWE-bench coding agents (file diffs, command repetition, test outcomes). The cross-agent transfer is within the same task distribution (SWE-bench), merely across agent implementations. No evaluation on non-coding agent tasks (WebArena, ToolBench, mathematical reasoning, data analysis). The paper claims the \"framework and probe architecture transfer directly\" to new domains but provides no evidence. The practical value proposition — \"design new LFs for your domain\" — is untested and the effort required is unquantified.\n68\t\n69\t5. **LLM-as-Judge comparison is underspecified.** One prompt template, two models, zero-shot and few-shot. The LLM-as-Judge literature (Zheng et al. 2024, which the paper itself cites conceptually) has shown that chain-of-thought reasoning, multi-turn evaluation, prompt optimization, and calibration substantially improve LLM judgment. The paper's claim that the probe outperforms LLM judges rests on a comparison against potentially suboptimal prompt engineering. At minimum, chain-of-thought and a structured rubric should be tested.\n70\t\n71\t### Moderate\n72\t\n73\t6. **LF temporal dynamics is descriptive, not methodological.** The observation that command_repetition leads by 1.8 steps and outcome_backpropagation lags by 2.1 steps is interesting but remains at the level of descriptive analysis. It does not feed back into the framework (e.g., a temporal label model that weights LFs by their temporal role) or change the probe architecture. The paper treats this as a contribution (Section 5.7, listed in the abstract's implicit contribution list), but it is an observation, not a method.\n74\t\n75\t7. **outcome_backpropagation LF introduces future information leakage.** For `exit_context` trajectories, ALL windows are labeled as stagnation regardless of actual content (line 166 of labeling_functions.py: `if trajectory.exit_status in (\"exit_context\",): return STAGNATION`). This means the probe may learn trajectory-position features as a proxy for genuine progress signals. The paper does not report what fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories or test sensitivity to this LF's design.\n76\t\n77\t8. **Venue fit concern.** The problem, LFs, evaluation, and downstream application are all deeply embedded in software engineering agent evaluation. The non-NLP baseline comparison (0.723 F1 for structural features) is positioned as evidence for NLP relevance, but the entire framework is SE-specific. A reviewer at an NLP venue could reasonably argue this belongs at ICSE, FSE, or ASE. The paper would benefit from at least a proof-of-concept on one non-SE agent domain.\n78\t\n79\t---\n80\t\n81\t## Questions for Authors\n82\t\n83\t1. **How does ProgressProbe differ from training a Process Reward Model (PRM) on agent trajectories?** A PRM trained on step-level outcome labels and aggregated over windows would serve a similar function. What is the specific advantage of the data-programming approach over direct PRM training?\n84\t\n85\t2. **Why was no label model used?** The paper applies majority voting but Snorkel's core contribution is a generative label model that estimates LF accuracies and correlations. Given that the LFs have very different coverage rates and temporal behaviors, a label model seems likely to improve label quality. Was this tried and found ineffective, or simply not attempted?\n86\t\n87\t3. **What fraction of training data comes from exit_context trajectories?** The outcome_backpropagation LF labels ALL windows as stagnation for these trajectories. If this is a large fraction, the probe may learn a trajectory-level signal (position + exit status) rather than window-level progress.\n88\t\n89\t4. **What embedding model is used?** The paper says \"a frozen sentence encoder\" with 1024 dimensions but never identifies it. Code inspection suggests Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B. This is a basic reproducibility failure. How sensitive are results to the embedding model choice?\n90\t\n91\t5. **Could active learning on the 400-window gold set achieve comparable results?** With human labels available, iteratively querying the most uncertain windows for annotation could potentially match the weak-supervision approach with fewer total labels and higher quality.\n92\t\n93\t6. **What is the concrete plan for extending to non-coding domains?** The paper claims the framework \"transfers directly\" but provides no evidence. What would the LFs look like for WebArena or mathematical reasoning?\n94\t\n95\t7. **Was a soft-match variant of Repeat-3 tested?** The current Repeat-3 baseline uses exact/near-exact string matching. An embedding-similarity-based repetition detector would be a fairer comparison and likely close part of the gap.\n96\t\n97\t---\n98\t\n99\t## Minor Issues\n100\t\n101\t- The paper describes a BiGRU with learned attention (Equations 2-3, Section 3.4) but code inspection reveals a unidirectional 2-layer GRU with last-hidden-state pooling and no attention mechanism. This code-paper discrepancy was flagged by R1 and is a serious concern. All attention-based interpretability claims (concentration coefficient 0.73, onset localization at steps 2-3) are unsupported if the actual model lacks attention.\n102\t\n103\t- All results are from a single seed (42). With margins of 2-7pp over baselines and a small gold set (400 windows, yielding ~+/-3-4pp bootstrap CI), multi-seed variance reporting is essential to assess statistical reliability.\n104\t\n105\t- The LF hold-one-out ablation (Table 6.3) retrains the probe on labels from 2 LFs, but the conf>=2 threshold changes meaning when there are only 2 LFs (conf>=2 means both agree, which is a stronger filter). This confounds the LF contribution with the label quality change.\n106\t\n107\t- Table 1 (gold set results) reports the probe at 0.862 but the abstract says 0.86 — minor rounding inconsistency.\n108\t\n109\t- The \"intervention paradox\" terminology is overwrought for what is simply the standard false-positive cost in early stopping. Framing it as a \"paradox\" suggests novelty where there is a well-known tradeoff.\n110\t\n111\t- Fleiss kappa of 0.724 is \"substantial\" agreement, but the paper should note that this reflects genuine task ambiguity — not all windows have a clear ground truth. The gold set itself has ~15% inherent noise.\n112\t\n113\t---\n114\t\n115\t## Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n116\t\n117\t### Justification\n118\t\n119\tThe paper addresses a real, practically important problem and presents an impressively thorough experimental program. The \"student exceeds teacher\" result, the cost-efficiency analysis, and the cross-agent transfer are genuine contributions to the agent monitoring space. The experimental design — particularly the human gold set to break circular evaluation, instance-level splitting, and comprehensive baselines — shows methodological maturity well above average.\n120\t\n121\tHowever, three issues prevent acceptance:\n122\t\n123\t**First, novelty is insufficient for a top venue.** The framework combines vanilla Snorkel majority voting (no label model, no temporal adaptation) with a standard GRU classifier. Neither component involves methodological innovation. The \"first to apply data programming to sequential trajectory data\" is a narrow priority claim weakened by the fact that the sequential aspect is handled entirely downstream, not in the data programming step itself. The LF temporal dynamics analysis — the most conceptually interesting finding — is descriptive only and does not feed back into the framework.\n124\t\n125\t**Second, related work is severely incomplete.** Ten references for a paper positioned at the intersection of agent monitoring, weak supervision, and sequential data analysis is far below the standard for a top venue. The absence of any engagement with process reward models, RL value functions, agent self-reflection methods (Reflexion, LATS), weak supervision advances beyond Snorkel, and noisy label learning creates a positioning vacuum. A reader cannot assess how ProgressProbe relates to these well-established alternatives.\n126\t\n127\t**Third, the domain specificity of both the LFs and the evaluation undermines the generality claims.** Cross-agent transfer within SWE-bench is a weak form of generalization. Without even one non-coding evaluation, the paper's contribution is confined to software engineering agent monitoring — a niche that may be better served by SE venues.\n128\t\n129\tThe code-paper architecture discrepancy noted by R1 is an additional concern that must be resolved. If the actual model is a simple GRU without attention, the architecture ablation showing attention adds +1.64pp and all interpretability claims based on attention weights are unsupported.\n130\t\n131\t### Path to Acceptance\n132\t\n133\tThe paper has a viable path if the authors: (1) implement a temporal label model that leverages LF leading/lagging dynamics, turning the descriptive finding into a methodological contribution; (2) expand related work to 30+ references with explicit positioning against PRMs, RL value functions, and agent self-reflection; (3) align code and paper on the architecture; (4) report multi-seed variance; (5) evaluate on at least one non-coding agent domain. The experimental infrastructure is already strong — the gap is in novelty, positioning, and rigor.\n134\t\n135\t### Confidence: 4/5 (High)\n136\t\n137\tI am familiar with the data programming, agent monitoring, and process reward model literatures. I am confident in my assessment of novelty and related work completeness. I am less certain about the code-paper discrepancy, which I rely on R1's inspection for.\n138\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01U2bXe4Pf8NggsEPqSUXT33","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"3132636e-02f3-4fb3-aece-9f841a32002c","timestamp":"2026-05-14T10:41:10.153Z"}
