{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011xU6Mf8X8TDghSdHnj898h","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_013FQNPJXGamZ18MqwnQcyiq","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"## R1 Review: Methods and Experiments\n\n### Summary\nThe paper proposes a Temporal Label Model (TLM), an HMM-based weak supervision aggregator that exploits leading/lagging temporal roles of labeling functions on sequential agent trajectory data, and ProgressProbe, a lightweight GRU classifier trained on TLM labels for detecting semantic stagnation in LLM agent trajectories. Evaluated on a 412-window human-annotated gold set, TLM improves over majority vote by 12.2pp and the probe achieves 0.858 macro-F1, outperforming LF oracle (+6.0pp) and LLM-as-judge (+4.5pp) at 690x lower latency, with graceful cross-agent transfer (SWE-agent to OpenHands, -6.7pp vs -21.6pp for LF oracle).\n\n### Strengths\n\n1. **Well-motivated problem formulation.** The paper clearly defines \"semantic stagnation\" as distinct from simple repetition loops and makes a convincing case that window-level classification over agent trajectories requires temporal modeling. The empirical demonstration that LFs exhibit systematic leading/lagging roles (Table 1) is a genuinely interesting finding.\n\n2. **Thorough experimental coverage.** The paper addresses nearly every anticipated reviewer concern with dedicated experiments: LF hold-one-out, outcome leakage sensitivity, encoder ablation, architecture ablation, data scaling, multi-seed stability, cross-agent transfer, LLM-as-judge comparison, and non-NLP structural baseline. This is unusually comprehensive.\n\n3. **Sound label model ladder.** The MV -> FlyingSquid -> TLM progression (Table 3) is cleanly designed and demonstrates that the improvement comes specifically from temporal modeling rather than just better correlation estimation. The BIC comparison (delta-BIC = -342.7) provides principled model selection evidence.\n\n4. **Compelling cross-agent transfer result.** The asymmetry between probe degradation (-6.7pp) and LF oracle degradation (-21.6pp) on OpenHands is the paper's strongest empirical claim. It demonstrates that the embedding-based probe genuinely abstracts away agent-specific surface patterns.\n\n5. **Practical early termination application.** The simulation on 487 held-out trajectories (Table 11) with explicit false termination rate reporting converts the detection capability into concrete cost savings, grounding the contribution.\n\n6. **Honest treatment of outcome leakage.** The hold-out analysis showing only -1.2pp drop when removing outcome_backpropagation, plus the last-3-windows-only variant (-0.4pp), adequately addresses the leakage concern.\n\n### Weaknesses\n\n1. **Circularity in LF temporal dynamics analysis (Table 1).** Stagnation \"onset\" is defined by the majority vote of the same LFs whose temporal roles are then measured relative to that onset. This is acknowledged internally but insufficiently discussed in the paper. If command_repetition dominates the vote (97.4% coverage), then \"stagnation onset\" is largely \"when command_repetition fires,\" making its measured lead (-1.82 steps) partially tautological. The Granger causality test (p=0.002) inherits this circularity. The paper should either (a) define onset using the gold set labels and re-measure temporal roles, or (b) use a leave-one-out onset definition where the LF being measured is excluded from the onset calculation.\n\n2. **Gold set is used for too many purposes simultaneously.** The 412-window gold set serves as: (i) the primary evaluation benchmark for all methods (Table 2), (ii) the validation target for TLM label quality (Table 3), (iii) the basis for LF hold-one-out ablation (Table 9), and (iv) the error analysis sample (Section 5.8). With no separate held-out gold test split, there is implicit selection pressure -- the TLM hyperparameters (or even the decision to use an HMM with 2 states) may have been tuned with knowledge of gold set performance. At N=412 the margin between TLM (0.841) and FlyingSquid (0.782) is ~24 windows, which is meaningful but not enormous. Confidence intervals or bootstrap significance tests on the gold set are absent throughout.\n\n3. **Implementation discrepancy between paper and code.** The paper describes (Section 3.5) \"a single-layer GRU (hidden=256) followed by attention-weighted aggregation,\" but the implementation uses a 2-layer GRU with no attention mechanism -- it uses the final hidden state directly (hidden[-1]). The training script default is num_layers=2. This is a significant paper-code misalignment that undermines reproducibility claims and raises the question of which architecture produced the reported numbers.\n\n4. **Missing statistical significance on main claims.** The paper reports multi-seed stability (0.919 +/- 0.011) and a paired t-test vs Repeat-3 (p=0.008), but no significance tests are reported for the central comparisons: TLM vs FlyingSquid on gold, probe vs LF oracle on gold, probe vs Claude CoT on gold. Given N=412, McNemar's test or paired bootstrap would be appropriate and necessary.\n\n5. **Conf>=2 vs gold set evaluation ambiguity.** The architecture ablation (Table 6) and encoder ablation (Table 8) are evaluated on conf>=2 test set (automatically labeled), while the main results (Table 2) are on the human gold set. These are different evaluation regimes with potentially different difficulty distributions. The paper should report architecture/encoder ablations on the gold set as well, or explicitly justify why conf>=2 is an adequate proxy.\n\n6. **Cross-agent evaluation ground truth is unclear.** The OpenHands evaluation reports F1 scores (Table 5), but against what ground truth? If against LF-generated labels on OpenHands data, this is problematic because the paper itself shows LFs degrade severely on OpenHands (command_repetition: 0.491, edit_churn: 0.385). If against human labels for the OpenHands 200-window set, this is not stated. The evaluation methodology for the cross-agent claim needs clarification.\n\n7. **LLM-as-judge cost comparison is misleading.** All LLM configurations show identical latency (2.1s) and cost ($4.82/1k) regardless of zero-shot/few-shot/CoT, which is implausible -- CoT generates substantially more output tokens and few-shot uses more input tokens. The 690x speedup claim should use per-configuration costs.\n\n### Questions for Authors\n\n1. How is stagnation onset defined in the temporal dynamics analysis (Table 1)? If it is defined by majority vote of the same LFs, have you measured temporal roles using gold-set-defined onsets as a robustness check?\n\n2. The code implements a 2-layer GRU with last-hidden-state pooling, while the paper describes a 1-layer GRU with attention pooling. Which architecture produced the reported numbers? Please clarify and ensure paper-code consistency.\n\n3. What is the ground truth for OpenHands F1 in Table 5? Is it human-annotated (200 windows), LF-generated, or something else? How were the OpenHands gold windows sampled?\n\n4. Were any hyperparameters of TLM (number of hidden states, emission model structure, EM initialization) selected with visibility into gold set performance? If so, what is the risk of overfitting to this small evaluation set?\n\n5. The test set has 73.4% progress windows vs 63.4% in train. How does this distribution shift affect the reported macro-F1? Have you evaluated with stratified splitting?\n\n6. Table 3 shows FlyingSquid and MV produce the same downstream probe F1 (0.831). Does this mean the probe is insensitive to label model quality below a certain threshold, or is this a coincidence?\n\n7. For the multi-seed experiment (0.919 +/- 0.011), the seeds are evaluated on conf>=2 test set. What is the multi-seed variance on the gold set?\n\n### Missing Experiments or Analysis\n\n1. **Bootstrap confidence intervals on gold set.** At N=412, the ranking of methods is sensitive to sampling. Report 95% CIs for all gold set F1 values to establish which differences are statistically significant.\n\n2. **Leave-one-out onset definition for temporal dynamics.** Re-measure Table 1 with onset defined excluding the LF being measured, to break the circularity.\n\n3. **Architecture ablation on gold set.** Table 6 uses conf>=2; report these numbers on the gold set to verify the ranking holds.\n\n4. **FlyingSquid sensitivity to LF temporal correlation.** FlyingSquid models pairwise correlations but not temporal structure. An ablation where FlyingSquid is given temporally-shuffled data (destroying sequential structure) vs original order would isolate the contribution of temporal modeling more cleanly.\n\n5. **Per-class analysis on cross-agent transfer.** Report stagnation-F1 and progress-F1 separately for OpenHands to understand whether the probe's transfer advantage is symmetric or class-specific.\n\n6. **Window overlap analysis.** With k=5 and stride=1 (implied), adjacent windows share 4/5 steps. The effective independence of the 54,809 windows is much lower than the raw count suggests. Report effective sample size or evaluate at stride=k.\n\n7. **TLM sensitivity to EM initialization.** HMM-EM is known to be sensitive to initialization. Report results across multiple random EM initializations.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n1. The paper claims 847K parameters (Section 3.5, Table 7), but a single-layer GRU with input=1024, hidden=256 has ~984K parameters (3 gates x (1024x256 + 256x256 + 256x2) + 256 classifier). Please verify.\n\n2. Table 2 mixes label model accuracy (MV, FlyingSquid, TLM rows) with classifier predictions (probe, baseline rows) in the same \"Gold F1\" column. This conflates two different evaluation types. Consider separating or adding a \"Type\" column.\n\n3. Reference [15] (Zhang et al. 2024, SpecRA) and [16] (Li et al. 2024, TrajAD) are \"arXiv preprint\" without specific arXiv IDs, raising verifiability concerns.\n\n4. The related work section is adequate but thin (~16 references). The data programming extension literature (e.g., work on temporal weak supervision in clinical/NLP domains) would strengthen the positioning.\n\n5. The \"semantic stagnation\" terminology is introduced without connection to existing agent failure taxonomies. A brief mapping to established categories (e.g., action cycling, goal drift) would help readers.\n\n6. Latency for label model rows in Table 2 is listed as \"---\" but TLM requires running 4 LFs plus HMM inference at test time, which has non-zero latency. This should be reported.\n\n### Confidence Score\n4\n\n### Preliminary Score\nScore: 4 — The paper presents a well-executed empirical study with a genuinely useful temporal label model contribution and strong cross-agent transfer results, but the circularity in the temporal dynamics analysis, paper-code architecture mismatch, missing significance tests on the gold set, and unclear cross-agent ground truth are substantive methodological concerns that need resolution before acceptance. If the authors can address the circularity (Q1), clarify the architecture (Q2), and add gold-set confidence intervals, this would move to a 5.","project":"agent_progress_probe","tier":"best","dream_id":"dream_20260514T095527","overall_score":"Weak Accept (4/6)"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":686,"cache_read_input_tokens":70618,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":686,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01SLbL3N6wstXn9xqWvryVSC","session_id":"f0157715-28cd-4094-a367-772100c08664","uuid":"5f069977-dd89-4842-82ec-331dc0c1e246"}
